The vendored C core
===================

SVXConnect-Qt compiles the SVXConnect-CLI core (reflector protocol, mTLS,
AES-GCM, Opus, jitter buffer, talkgroup manager, control FIFO, run lock, status
export) into a static library and wraps it in Qt. In git that core is a
submodule at third_party/svxconnect-cli.

A git submodule does NOT survive dpkg-source: it records a gitlink, not files,
so a source package built from a plain `git archive` or a `debian/` checkout
contains an empty directory and the build fails on the CMake guard in
CMakeLists.txt. Launchpad and most autobuilders never fetch submodules either.

The core is therefore VENDORED into the .orig tarball. Regenerate it with:

    packaging/make-orig-tarball.sh

which exports both trees at their committed revisions and writes
../svxconnect-qt_<version>.orig.tar.xz. debian/rules then points CLI_DIR at the
in-tree copy.

The vendored core is covered by its own stanza in debian/copyright, together
with the miniaudio header it in turn vendors.

Why the core is static, not a shared library
--------------------------------------------
It is not a stable ABI and is not intended to be one — its headers use _Atomic
and are consumed through a hand-written C++-clean shim (src/core/svxcore.h)
whose prototypes are checked against upstream at build time by
tools/shim_check.c. Publishing it as a shared library would freeze an interface
that upstream deliberately keeps free to change.
